lternatives\" — a conjecture). RSS decides — by naming a category — that whatever shares booking and revision but differs in a field is a \"near-duplicate,\" full stop.\n\nDistinguishing case: the source also entertains (via \"different bookings need not share the same choice\") the possibility that two records with *the same booking and same revision* but *different kinds* are \"different records tied at a greatest revision.\" The Lean file encodes that as `alternatives_at_greatest` with `¬ exact_duplicate`. RSS encodes it as near-duplication. Both are expressible; only the Lean file states the tie/supersession residual as a *consequence*, whereas RSS asserts it as a category. The lost distinction is that \"not exactly identical\" is not a positive category in the source, but RSS makes it one.\n\n**(b) The source's \"tilt\" is hedged, but RSS's \"reading\" is a positive choice-token, not a hedge.**\n\nFrozen prose:\n\n> \"The snapshot description tilts toward the first: a higher revision supersedes lower revisions, and expiry can make a hold reserve zero. It does not say expiry removes the supersession. So the first is better supported, but the second remains a live conjecture…\"\n\nThe expression carries this as:\n\n> \"The source's own phrase 'the snapshot description tilts toward the first' is carried as the source's own hedge, not upgraded to a commitment, and RSS preserves that hedge by treating the tilt as a reading preference rather than a fact.\"\n\nThe distinction lost is between **(i)** a *reasoner's tilt* — an epistemic preference about which of two conjectures is better supported by the text — and **(ii)** a *reading* — a policy RSS treats as one of two equally available options. The source's \"tilt\" is an indication about *probative weight*; RSS's \"reading\" is a *parametric slot*. Treating the tilt as a reading preference converts a claim about evidence into a claim about an option list. The prose-escape even registers this as an interpretive choice (\"could be contested\"), but the *distinction* — tilt vs. reading-option — is not preserved as a distinction; it's collapsed into one kind of thing, \"reading.\"\n\n**(c) Global availability \"not even convex\"**\n\nFrozen prose:\n\n> \"If different bookings have unresolved alternatives, availability is not a number but a set or an interval, and possibly not even a convex one.\"\n\nThe Lean file *cannot* express non-convexity of a set of possible global availability values, because it only computes lower and upper bounds over `Nat`-derived `Int`s, per booking. That is a real expressive limit (the Lean file itself does not flag this one explicitly). RSS, however, could have preserved the non-convexity distinction. It doesn't; it converts it to:\n\n> \"availability is not a number but a set or an interval, and possibly not even a convex one. … RSS represents both as readings.\"\n\nBut \"RSS represents both as readings\" does not preserve *non-convexity* — it preserves *two bounds* (a pessimistic and an optimistic reading). The distinction between \"an interval with possible gaps\" and \"an interval\" vanishes: the expression announces \"possibly not even a convex one\" in the voice of the source, then reduces to min/max readings. The substantive claim \"possibly not even convex\" is stated and then not carried into RSS's vocabulary; RSS offers only \"lower bound\" and \"upper bound\" readings, which are convex by construction.\n\n## 2. Introduced commitments\n\n**(a) A commitment to a first-person voice that RSS's own vocabulary contradicts.**\n\nThe expression opens:\n\n> \"The warehouse snapshot is a collection of rival stories. **I will not compute one answer for it.** I will say what it is, what can be said about it in the vocabulary of stories, viewpoints, readings, and accounts, and where that vocabulary runs out.\"\n\nIts own independent reader flags this:\n\n> \"The expression begins with a first-person declaration ('I will not compute one answer') that sits uneasily with RSS's own framing, which is about accounts, not voices. If the 'I' is an account, it is not named; if it is the semantics speaking, the semantics has no first person.\"\n\nThis is a *reader-visible* introduced commitment: a stance the source does not take and RSS does not license. RSS's primitives have \"account\" and \"viewpoint,\" not \"I.\" The source's own voice is third-person and about people (\"People may disagree about whether uncertainty should be retained\"). The \"I\" imports an agent that neither the source nor RSS names. It is small, but it is a commitment not derivable from the carrier.\n\n**(b) A commitment that the set-of-reservations is \"more informative than the sign\" — promoted from source conjecture to RSS assertion.**\n\nSource:\n\n> \"A conjecture: the sign of availability is less important than the set of reservations that produce it…\"\n\nNote: *conjecture*. The expression writes:\n\n> \"RSS: availability is a derived quantity that can be negative under a reading; the sign is a signal, not an error; the set of reservations is more informative than the sign. The conjecture that the sign of availability is less important than the set of reservations that produce it—RSS accepts this as a conjecture about a projection.\"\n\nTwo competing framings appear in adjacent sentences: (i) RSS *states as its own* \"the set of reservations is more informative than the sign,\" and (ii) the source's conjecture is \"accepted as a conjecture about a projection.\" The reader of the expression receives both the promotion (\"RSS: … more informative than the sign\") and the demotion (\"accepted as a conjecture\"). If RSS's own statement is not a conjecture, then the source's conjecture has been upgraded to a semantic-class assertion; if it is a conjecture, then the \"RSS:\" prefix is misusing the semantics class name. The reader's independent section notes this same stress:\n\n> \"Whether any single-number availability is authorized. The expression says a projection is 'a loss of information'… Whether that is a defect or a feature is left to accounts.\"\n\n**(c) A commitment that \"criticisms and refutations are local to accounts and do not force global agreement\" is fit for the source.**\n\nThe source's third disagreement asks what a criticism *targets*; it does not commit to any locality thesis about criticisms. RSS asserts:\n\n> \"Refutations are local to accounts and do not force global agreement.\"\n\nThe expression repeats this and then applies it. But the frozen source contains, verbatim:\n\n> \"False or incomplete proposals are not discarded; they are part of the evidence about how people understand the system.\"\n\nThat is a claim about *retention*, not locality. The expression silently substitutes an *account-locality thesis* for the source's *retention*-thesis. Whether refutations are account-local is a substantive philosophical commitment about disagreement; the source does not make it, and the expression does, without flagging it as added.\n\n**(d) A commitment that RSS is the correct carrier for the source — though the packet already fixed that choice.**\n\nThe reader's section \"Against the non-Lean stance\" says:\n\n> \"non-Lean semantics is not automatically freer than a Lean translation; it is differently constrained. It cannot state a theorem, cannot check a proof, and cannot mechanically distinguish a refutation from a contradiction-in-prose.\"\n\nThe expression never argues for the choice; it simply performs it. Whether that is a \"commitment\" or a \"context\" is arguable (the packet selects the carrier), but the *consequences* of the choice — e.g., refutation becomes one of three prose-defined moves, none mechanically checkable — are not registered by the expression. RSS's own description of refutation is where this becomes visible:\n\n> \"A refutation is one of: (a) a counterexample within an account's own viewpoint; (b) a derivation of inconsistency within an account's own reading; or (c) a demonstration that a conjecture depends on an assumption that another account rejects.\"\n\nCompare the Lean file, which is more modest: a refutation is a snapshot together with `¬ c snap`. RSS's (b) and (c) have no mechanical analogue; they are refutation-shaped speech acts. The expression adopts RSS's wider sense without noting the loss of the Lean sense.\n\n## 3. Vanished uncertainty\n\n**(a) The source's \"possibly not even a convex one\"** — stated, then dropped (see 1(c)). The uncertainty about *shape* of the availability set vanishes into a min/max story.\n\n**(b) The source's worry about \"physics of possibility smuggling.\"**\n\nSource:\n\n> \"A criticism: once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.\"\n\nThe expression converts this into:\n\n> \"The criticism that once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain—RSS accepts this criticism as a criticism of a specific projection, not of the semantics itself.\"\n\nThe uncertainty in the original is about whether the *move to possible worlds* was legitimate in the first place. The expression reclassifies the criticism as targeting a *projection* — i.e., it localizes a criticism that was, in the source, potentially global. That is a substantive downgrade of the criticism (see also §5).\n\n**(c) Source's ambiguity about whether the snapshot is \"the union of all deliveries, or the intersection, or one replica's view.\"** The expression's reader's view flags:\n\n> \"Whether the bag is union, intersection, or single-replica view. The expression says RSS 'does not decide,' and that this is represented as a viewpoint-relative matter. But it also says the snapshot is 'described as replicated,' without deciding which replication semantics the frozen source actually intends.\"\n\nThe uncertainty is *named* but not *carried as an open question in RSS's vocabulary*. RSS has no primitive for \"the source's replication intent is unresolved.\" \"Viewpoint\" is defined as (coordinate, bag) — but the bag is just a bag. The uncertainty about what *kind* of bag it is has nowhere to live except in a prose comment. It is named, not encoded.\n\n**(d) The `prose-escape` says \"no Lean fragment is being obstructed\" — but this is itself a commitment, not an uncertainty.** The reader notes:\n\n> \"The prose-escape block's claim that no Lean fragment is being obstructed cannot be verified from the expression alone, and it should not be treated as a proof that the source contained no Lean — only as a commitment of the expression not to represent one.\"\n\nThis is a vanished uncertainty: the expression converts an *unverifiable-hence-open* question (\"did the source contain Lean fragments?\") into an *asserted absence* (\"no Lean fragment is being obstructed\"). The Lean file's presence in the packet certainly means the packet contained a Lean expression, even if *that* Lean expression was a proposed language rather than a translation of a fragment. Whether the frozen source *contains* Lean is a separate question from whether the *translation attempt* is obstructing one.\n\n## 4. Broken dependencies\n\n**(a) The relation between `ReservationRule` and `possible_reservations` is broken by declaration order in the Lean file.**\n\nIn the Lean file:\n\n```\ndef rules_disagree (r1 r2 : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  ∃ b, possible_reservations_with r1 snap b ≠ possible_reservations_with r2 snap b\n\n/-- Reservation possibilities parameterized by a rule. -/\ndef possible_reservations_with (rule : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  ...\n```\n\n`rules_disagree` references `possible_reservations_with` before the latter is declared. In Lean 4, this order would produce an \"unknown constant\" error unless forward declaration or reordering is used. This is a defect *in the Lean file*, which is part of the frozen corpus but not the selected carrier. It is still worth recording: the *source*'s dependency (\"two rules disagree on a snapshot iff their possible-reservation lists differ for some booking\") is broken at the level of the file. However, since the selected carrier is the non-Lean RSS, this defect is *not* the fault of the selected language; it is a translator/compiler-bridge defect in the packet. Recording a repair without installing it: reorder the two `def`s, or declare `possible_reservations_with` first.\n\n**(b) RSS declares `provenance` a field of a story, but does not make it a field of a delivery.**\n\nRSS's Primitives say:\n\n> \"A story has a booking, a revision, a kind (hold or release), a quantity, an expiry, and a **provenance** (which delivery or set of deliveries it is drawn from).\"\n> \"A **Bag**: an unordered collection of deliveries.\"\n\nBut a *delivery* is defined only as \"an event that makes a record available.\" A record's fields are booking, revision, kind, quantity, expiry. So provenance has no field-content to be drawn from — it can only point to a delivery, which is an event, not a record. Then:\n\n> \"**Exact duplication**: two deliveries are exact duplicates if all record fields and provenance are equal.\"\n\nBut provenance is defined as \"which delivery or set of deliveries [the story] is drawn from\" — that is, provenance is a property of a *story*, not a *delivery*. The definition of exact duplication therefore refers to a property that deliveries do not have. This is a broken dependency: the primitive \"delivery\" lacks the field that \"exact duplication\" quantifies over, and which \"story\" has.\n\nThe expression compounds this: it says\n\n> \"RSS: exact duplicates are inert in all readings. Near-duplicates—same booking and revision, differing in kind or quantity or expiry—are not covered by this statement…\"\n\nNo mention of provenance in this restatement. So the very field that RSS made a primitive to distinguish \"exact\" from \"near\" disappears when the distinction is applied. The reader's section catches this obliquely:\n\n> \"**That 'authority' is the right word for what exact duplicates fail to add.** 'Authority does not accumulate by repetition' is a gloss on 'duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority,' but the meaning of 'authority' in the source is not defined in the expression.\"\n\n**(c) \"Computing the availability\" — RSS's own primitives force a computation route that its own noncomputability refuses.**\n\nRSS says:\n\n> \"Availability is a derived quantity that can be negative under a reading … the set of reservations is more informative than the sign.\"\n\nBut deriving availability requires *summing reservations*, which requires *selecting* a reservation per booking, which requires *a reading*. RSS's noncomputability says no reading is selected. So \"derived quantity\" is a misdescription: under RSS, availability is a *derived-per-reading* quantity. The expression does hedge (\"under a reading\"), but the phrase \"is a derived quantity\" does not. The Lean file at least treats availability as a function `Snapshot → List Booking → Int` (an actual derivation), not as a *per-reading* quantity. The RSS substitution breaks a dependency: what was a function of a snapshot in the Lean carrier becomes a function of a viewpoint-and-reading pair in RSS, and no formal slot replaces the old one.\n\n## 5. Blocked criticism\n\n**(a) The criticism about the \"possible worlds\" smuggled notion is *localized* to a projection.**\n\nSource:\n\n> \"A criticism: once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.\"\n\nThe criticism as originally stated is *directed at the representational move itself* — arguably against the very idea of representing alternatives as a set of possible reservations. The expression *re-scopes* the criticism to a projection rather than the representation:\n\n> \"RSS accepts this criticism as a criticism of a specific projection, not of the semantics itself.\"\n\nThis blocks the criticism at its most potent level: a criticism that RSS's primitive vocabulary — story, viewpoint, alternative compatibility — already traffics in possible-world talk, and hence cannot avoid the smuggled notion, is redirected to \"a specific projection\" and thereby neutralized by the semantics' own partition (projections are losses; not-privileged). A reader who wants to say \"the whole RSS apparatus trades in smuggled possibility\" finds the expression has already declined that reading. The source's criticism, read uncharitably to RSS, is blocked.\n\n**(b) The criticism \"capacity may be misleading\" is localized to the primitive \"coordinate.\"**\n\nSource:\n\n> \"A criticism: if availability is allowed to be negative, then capacity is not a hard constraint, and the word 'capacity' may be misleading; it is more like a soft target or a reporting threshold.\"\n\nExpression:\n\n> \"RSS carries this as a criticism of the primitive 'coordinate,' which is a parameter of a viewpoint, not an absolute fact.\"\n\nThe source's criticism is about *a word and a concept in the system description*. The expression re-locates it to *a definitional primitive of RSS*. Whether that move is a faithful carrying-forward is arguable — but it has the effect of making the criticism sound like a complaint about RSS's choice of primitive name rather than a substantive objection to the underlying model. The reader's section names this kind of move:\n\n> \"**Against the treatment of negativity.** … The expression concedes this criticism … but treats it as a criticism of the primitive, not of the reconstruction.\"\n\n**(c) The criticism \"any single-number availability is a projection that discards information\" is promoted from conjecture to fact about projections, closing off its own contestability.**\n\nSource:\n\n> \"A conjecture: any single-number availability is a projection that discards information, and different projections can disagree even when they respect the snapshot.\"\n\nExpression:\n\n> \"The conjecture that any single-number availability is a projection that discards information—RSS states this as a fact about projections, not a conjecture.\"\n\nThe reader flags this:\n\n> \"**That 'any single-number availability is a projection that discards information' — RSS states this as a fact about projections, not a conjecture.**\"\n\nOnce it becomes a definitional fact about projections, the *criticism* — that non-projection might also discard information, or that projections might not always discard the information the source cares about — is blocked from being raised against RSS's own framing. The definitional status immunizes the claim.\n\n**(d) Source's phrase \"keeping these disagreements visible is not a failure of the reconstruction; it is the reconstruction's only honest form\" is *endorsed* rather than held open.**\n\nSource: a reconstructive claim.\n\nExpression: repeats the sentence and frames the *whole non-Lean apparatus* as the representation of this claim (\"In RSS, this is represented by inquiry states…\"). Reader's section:\n\n> \"A critic could say this is a rhetorical move: by calling visibility the only honest form, the expression immunizes itself against demands for resolution.\"\n\nThat is a blocked criticism in the specific sense: the criticism that the expression is *not* honest because it refuses to project is ruled out by the expression's framing of \"only honest form.\" The framing is a rhetorical device that forecloses the criticism before it can be raised.\n\n## 6. Where is the defect? (attribution)\n\nPartly the defects are in the **translator** (the expression) and partly in the **selected language** (RSS), partly in the **compiler bridge** (the Lean file's declaration-order issue), and partly in the **reader's section** (the source-text-withheld reading).\n\n- **Translator (expression)**: the \"I\" voice; the promotion of RSS-owned statements amidst conjectures; the re-scoping of criticisms to projections and primitives; the elision of non-convexity; the omission of provenance from the exact/near-duplicate restatement.\n- **Selected language (RSS)**: the broken dependency of provenance; the missing per-reading availability slot; the absence of a primitive for \"source's replication intent is unresolved\"; the substitution of an account-locality thesis for a retention-thesis; the reification of \"near-duplicate\" as a category.\n- **Compiler bridge (Lean file)**: the forward-reference bug in `rules_disagree`.\n- **Reader**: accurately identifies most of the above but also *itself* suggests that the question \"did the source contain Lean fragments?\" can be settled by the prose-escape block, then immediately retracts. The reader is careful, but the section \"Source quotations visible in the expression\" implies the reader has access to the expression's quotations of the source without access to the source — a fact which the reader notes. This is a *reader* problem, not an expression or language problem.\n\nThe Lean file's own header explicitly declares its expressive limits: replication, physical resource sharing, provenance, per-replica time. RSS claims to *add* these as primitives. The comparison of the two carriers would be a useful appendix to the packet: the Lean file says what it cannot express; RSS says what it can express but *does not say* — for provenance, for replication intent, for non-convexity — that these primitives are themselves incomplete. \"Provenance\" without a mechanism for comparing provenances is a word, not a primitive. That is a defect of the *selected language*, and the expression does not repair it.\n\n## 7. Disposition\n\nUnresolved. The principal defects are: the promotion of source-framed conjectures into RSS-owned assertions (§2(b),(c), §5(c)); the localization of criticisms to projections/definitions with a neutralizing effect (§5(a),(b)); the loss of the source's \"possibly not even convex\" (unrepresentable in Lean, dropped in RSS); the provenance break in RSS (a defect of the selected language, which the expression did not repair); and the Lean carrier's forward-reference bug (a translator/bridge defect). The most substantive prose objections — against smuggling possibility, against the \"only honest form\" rhetorical immunizations, and against the loss of a mechanically checkable refutation — remain admissible as prose objections even though RSS cannot encode the criticism of itself. A conflicting interpretation is possible: one could argue that RSS's \"reading\" primitive *is* the way the source's tilted-but-not-settled choice is preserved, and that the expression's classification of the tilt as a reading is an enhancement, not a defect. I record that possibility without choosing it."}